## Concept Overview
LRU (Least Recently Used) cache combines HashMap for fast key lookup and DoublyLinkedList for maintaining access order. When cache is full, least recently used items are evicted.

## Implementation Details
- **HashMap**: Maps keys to Node references for O(1) lookup
- **DoublyLinkedList**: Maintains access order with head (most recent) and tail (least recent)
- **Thread Safety**: Use ReentrantReadWriteLock - read lock for get(), write lock for put()
- **Eviction**: Remove tail node when capacity exceeded

## Code Example
```java
public class LRUCache<K, V> {
    private final Map<K, Node<K, V>> map;
    private final DoublyLinkedList<K, V> list;
    private final int capacity;
    private final ReadWriteLock lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();

    public V get(K key) {
        lock.readLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> node = map.get(key);
            if (node != null) {
                list.moveToHead(node);
                return node.value;
            }
            return null;
        } finally {
            lock.readLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}
```

## Common Pitfalls
- **Race Conditions**: Forgetting proper synchronization can corrupt the linked list
- **Memory Leaks**: Not removing references when evicting nodes
- **Performance**: Using synchronized blocks instead of ReadWriteLock reduces concurrency
- **Edge Cases**: Handling null keys/values and capacity of 0

## Why Asked
Tests dynamic programming skills and understanding of combinatorial counting
## Key Concepts
Dynamic programming, combinatorial counting, recurrence relations
## Code Example
```
function countWays(target) {
  const dp = new Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (let i = 1; i <= target; i++) {
    for (let die = 1; die <= 6; die++) {
      if (i >= die) dp[i] += dp[i - die];
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```
