## Why Asked
Tests understanding of graph traversal fundamentals and algorithm selection
## Key Concepts
Queue vs stack implementation, time complexity O(V+E), space complexity differences, use cases
## Code Example
```
// BFS
function bfs(graph, start) {
  const queue = [start];
  const visited = new Set();
  while (queue.length) {
    const node = queue.shift();
    if (!visited.has(node)) {
      visited.add(node);
      queue.push(...graph[node]);
    }
  }
}

// DFS
function dfs(graph, start) {
  const stack = [start];
  const visited = new Set();
  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    if (!visited.has(node)) {
      visited.add(node);
      stack.push(...graph[node]);
    }
  }
}
```
## Follow-up Questions
How to implement recursively? What about cycle detection? Time vs space trade-offs?

## Approach

This problem combines bit manipulation with in-place partitioning:

### Step 1: Find the Unique Element
Use XOR operation on all elements. Since `a ^ a = 0` and `a ^ 0 = a`, all paired elements cancel out, leaving only the unique element.

```javascript
let unique = 0;
for (let num of arr) {
  unique ^= num;
}
```

### Step 2: Partition Around Unique Element
Use three-way partitioning (similar to Dutch National Flag):
- Elements less than unique go left
- Unique element in middle
- Elements greater than unique go right

```javascript
let low = 0, mid = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
while (mid <= high) {
  if (arr[mid] < unique) {
    [arr[low], arr[mid]] = [arr[mid], arr[low]];
    low++; mid++;
  } else if (arr[mid] > unique) {
    [arr[mid], arr[high]] = [arr[high], arr[mid]];
    high--;
  } else {
    mid++;
  }
}
```

### Step 3: Sort Pairs
Within each partition, pairs are already together. Use counting sort or simply swap pairs into position.

**Time Complexity:** O(n)
**Space Complexity:** O(1)
